## Why This Is Asked

Tests the ability to design safe, evolvable event schemas in a multi-tenant CQRS+ES system, and to plan migrations that avoid data leakage while preserving historical integrity.

## Key Concepts

- Event versioning per tenant
- Backward-compatible schema evolution
- Read-model migration via replay
- Tenant isolation and per-tenant outbox

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal versioned event envelope (conceptual)
class EventEnvelope {
  constructor(tenantId, version, type, payload) {
    this.tenantId = tenantId;
    this.version = version;
    this.type = type;
    this.payload = payload;
  }
}
```

```javascript
// Projection sketch (conceptual)
function project(event, readModel, tenantRuleStore) {
  const rule = tenantRuleStore.get(event.tenantId, event.version);
  // Apply logic based on rule to compute new totals
  readModel.update(event.tenantId, event.payload, rule);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test migration correctness across tenants with varying rule histories?
- What metrics would you instrument to detect cross-tenant data leakage during replay?

## Why This Is Asked

Contextualizes real-world constraints of scalable stores with per-instrument sharding and cross-region replication. Focuses on practical trade-offs rather than theory.

## Key Concepts

- Dynamic shard management and hot-spot mitigation
- Multi-region replication with consistency guarantees
- Live shard rebalancing with in-flight data handling
- Disaster recovery via PITR and WAL strategies

## Code Example

```javascript
function rebalance(oldShard, newShard){
  pauseWrites(oldShard);
  migrateData(oldShard, newShard);
  replayInFlight(oldShard, newShard);
  updateRouting(oldShard, newShard);
  resumeWrites(oldShard);
}
```

