## Why This Is Asked

This question tests practical replay-protection patterns in multi-tenant SSO architectures, along with real-world token normalization from OIDC and SAML protocols, JWKS key rotation, and designing scalable validation endpoints.

## Key Concepts

- Token validation across OIDC and SAML standards
- Per-tenant JWKS rotation and SAML metadata caching
- Nonce-based replay protection with time-to-live (TTL) enforcement
- Internal JWT issuance including tenant_id, user_id, scopes, and nonce

## Code Example

```javascript
// Issue internal token with nonce (Node.js)
const jwt = require('jsonwebtoken');

async function issueInternalToken({ tenant_id, user_id, scopes, nonce }) {
  const payload = {
    tenant_id,
    sub: user_id,
    scopes,
    nonce,
    iat: Math.floor(Date.now() / 1000),
    exp: Math.floor(Date.now() / 1000) + 300 // 5-minute expiry
  };
  return jwt.sign(payload, getInternalSigningKey(tenant_id), { algorithm: 'RS256' });
}

// Validate nonce during token validation
async function validateAndConsumeNonce(tenant_id, nonce) {
  const key = `nonce:${tenant_id}:${nonce}`;
  const exists = await redis.setnx(key, '1');
  if (!exists) {
    throw new Error('Replay detected: nonce already used');
  }
  await redis.expire(key, 300); // Match token TTL
  return true;
}

// /validate endpoint - lightweight claim extraction
app.post('/validate', async (req, res) => {
  const { token } = req.body;
  const [header] = token.split('.');
  const decodedHeader = JSON.parse(Buffer.from(header, 'base64'));
  const tenant_id = decodedHeader.tenant_id;

  const claims = await extractClaimsWithoutFullDecode(token, tenant_id);
  return res.json({ valid: true, claims });
});
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of practical ES+CQRS integration across microservices with a lightweight saga.

## Key Concepts
- Event Sourcing: immutable event log
- CQRS: separate read model for queries
- Saga/Orchestrator: cross-service workflow with compensations

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal orchestrator sketch
function handleEvent(event) {
  switch (event.type) {
    case 'PostCreated':
      publish('ModerationRequested', { postId: event.data.postId });
      break;
    case 'ModerationResult':
      if (event.data.result === 'Approved') {
        publish('PostPublished', { postId: event.data.postId });
        publish('NotifyFollowers', { postId: event.data.postId });
      } else {
        publish('PostRejected', { postId: event.data.postId, reason: event.data.reason });
      }
      break;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you implement idempotent event processing in the Moderation service?
- What are the trade-offs of using a separate read model vs. a consolidated repository for listings of visible posts?

## Why This Is Asked
Assesses ability to design distributed workflow using sagas, CQRS, and event-sourcing in real-world microservices; probes handling of failure modes, idempotency, compensations, and consistency guarantees.

## Key Concepts
- Saga orchestration vs choreography
- Event store and projections for CQRS
- Compensating actions and timeouts
- Idempotent handlers and outbox pattern

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code: saga orchestrator pattern
class CheckoutSaga {
  async start(order) {
    await dispatch('ReserveInventory', order);
  }
  async onEvent(event) {
    if (event.type === 'InventoryReserved') await dispatch('CapturePayment', event.order);
    else if (event.type === 'PaymentCaptured') await dispatch('ScheduleShipment', event.order);
    else if (event.type === 'ShipmentScheduled') complete(event.order);
    else if (event.type.endsWith('Failed')) compensate(event.order);
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- What are the trade-offs between orchestration and choreography in this scenario?
- How would you test the saga under partial failures and timeouts?

## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates the ability to architect a sophisticated multi-protocol API gateway that addresses enterprise-grade requirements including tenant isolation, dynamic feature management, and operational excellence at scale.

## Key Concepts

- Multi-protocol API gateway design supporting REST, GraphQL, and gRPC
- Redis-based distributed feature flags with tenant-specific configurations
- Centralized OpenAPI specification management with versioning and deprecation strategies
- GraphQL schema stitching integrating REST and gRPC services with lazy loading and streaming
- Multi-layer data isolation through API-level and database-level policy enforcement
- Per-tenant observability using OpenTelemetry and advanced deployment patterns (canary, blue-green)

## Code Example

```javascript
// Tenant-aware GraphQL field resolver with REST backend integration
const resolvers = {
  Query: {
    userData: async (parent, { id }, context) => {
      const { tenantId, featureFlags } = context;

      // Check feature flag before proceeding
      if (!await featureFlags.isEnabled('userData', tenantId)) {
        throw new Error('Feature not available for tenant');
      }

      // Fetch from REST backend with tenant isolation
      return fetch(`/api/v1/tenants/${tenantId}/users/${id}`);
    }
  }
};

// Per-tenant OpenAPI specification fetch
class TenantSpecGenerator {
  async generateTenantSpec(tenantId, version = 'latest') {
    const baseSpec = await this.getBaseSpec();
    const tenantConfig = await this.getTenantConfig(tenantId);

    return {
      ...baseSpec,
      servers: [{ url: `https://${tenantId}.api.example.com` }],
      'x-tenant-id': tenantId,
      'x-version': version,
      paths: this.filterPathsForTenant(baseSpec.paths, tenantConfig)
    };
  }
}
```
